# Refresh component upstream commits

`azldev comp refresh-upstream-commit` (`comp` is an alias for `component`) resolves one or more
components at the distro snapshot and writes normal component TOML under
`{{ .UpstreamCommitsDir }}/`.

The project config must include `{{ .UpstreamCommitsDir }}/*.toml` **before**
the component-specific TOML configuration so subsequent commands load the
generated commit pins and the component definition supplies the remaining
`spec` fields. If `--upstream-commits-dir` selects another directory, change
the include pattern to match while preserving that ordering.

The command uses the fully merged component configuration. It resolves and
writes a pin only when the selected component's effective `spec.type` is
`upstream`; for other source types, it removes any existing generated pin
without contacting an upstream provider.
It loads configuration permissively so a stale generated pin cannot block
cleanup after a component is removed or converted to another source type.
Other configuration validation failures are still reported as warnings.

## When to run `refresh-upstream-commit`

| Situation | Run `refresh-upstream-commit`? |
| --- | --- |
| Adding a new upstream component | **Yes** — first, to generate its upstream commit TOML |
| Finalizing overlay, build config, or metadata changes | No — these do not affect the resolved commit |
| Changing source resolution (commit pin, upstream distro/version, or snapshot) | **Yes** — also mid-workflow (see below) |
| Iterating on overlays / build config / metadata | No — use `render --allow-uncommitted` while inputs are uncommitted |
| Just reading or building existing components | No |

Refresh a single component with `-p <name>`. Use `-a` (all components) only for
coordinated mass refreshes (e.g. a new distro snapshot) or when investigating
commit drift across many components — it is slow. For day-to-day work use `-p`.
Add `-O json` for machine-readable output when debugging.

## Finalizing component changes

Select this workflow by listing the component's exact name in
`lockless-components.txt` beside the top-level `azldev.toml`.
Alternatively, `azldev comp make-lockless` adds selected exact names if needed
and runs this refresh pipeline. It accepts `-p`, `-g`, `-a`, or positional names,
preserves existing list contents and lock files, and refreshes already-listed components.
A refresh failure leaves the updated list in place; fix the failure and rerun.
Refresh skips unlisted components and preserves their generated TOML files.

Commit component inputs before rendering. For upstream components, rendering
compares the fully parsed committed TOML at the last render commit and at HEAD;
uncommitted generated pins do not count as a committed upstream change.

Before any upstream checkout or component preparation, rendering requires a
clean project Git repository: staged changes, unstaged tracked changes, and
untracked files anywhere in the repository block the whole invocation.
Git-ignored files are excluded. Use `--allow-uncommitted` for intentional dirty
renders; `--check-only` and `--no-git-commit` do not bypass this check.
The override does not change committed-history carryover or failure recovery,
which can discard uncommitted files in the component output directory.
Locked and local components do not trigger this check.

```sh
git add <changed-component-config-and-source-paths> \
	{{ .UpstreamCommitsDir }}/<name>.toml
git commit -m "fix(<name>): ..."
azldev comp render -p <name>
```

Rendering clones only the effective upstream distro's `dist-git-branch`, keeping
its full history rather than making a shallow clone. If the last render's
committed TOML resolves to a different branch and the pins differ, rendering
fetches that historical branch before comparing commits, without switching the
working checkout.

With `build.emit-upstream-provenance`, rendering captures Fedora provenance
immediately after checking out the pinned upstream commit, before
release/changelog carryover or bumping. Host `rpmautospec calculate-release`
runs here only for an upstream `%autorelease` whose `fedora_upstream_release`
is not already supplied by `build.defines`. The captured values are reused
when writing the macros file; that step never repeats the provenance query.

After reading render history and collecting upstream changes, rendering prepares
the changelog before Release, then writes the macros file, applies overlays, and
updates `sources` before comparison and the final `rpmdev-bumpspec`.
Release edits preserve the existing spaces and tabs after `Release:`.
Version detection and carryover use the upstream spec before overlays.
Overlays can override the prepared release and changelog.
Carryover is not repeated after overlays, even if they change the version.
Rendering compares output before the final release bump; `--check-only` stops before that bump.
Unchanged lockless upstream renders require `--no-change-rebuild`; otherwise rendering errors
without replacing existing output. The flag records host `date -Is` output in
`.no-change-rebuild` before bumping, only when no changes were detected. Comparison
ignores this root marker in both existing output and the working upstream checkout.
Lockless failures never write `RENDER_FAILED`. Before replacement, output is
untouched; failures after replacement starts clear the component directory and
use Git to restore committed output, or leave no directory if none existed.
Recovery discards uncommitted changes in that directory and keeps no backups.
The comparison ignores the root `.git` without traversing or removing it.

Changelog handling precedes release preparation and is independent of version
changes. Static specs retain the committed inline `%changelog` section.
An initial `%autorelease` render generates the complete upstream changelog on
the host. Legacy-marker output supplies its committed inline `%changelog` body
as the working `changelog`, removing leading and trailing `##` comment lines
while preserving interior comments and whitespace. Modern autorelease output
replaces the working `changelog` with the committed file byte-for-byte.
A missing committed `changelog` fails rendering.

Before overlays, rendering compares the upstream spec's `Version:` with the
committed spec, independently of upstream pin changes.
Static release carryover runs only when committed output exists and the version
is unchanged. Otherwise the working static release
and `baserelease` definition are unchanged.
There is no later restoration of the original upstream release.

For static specs, release carryover checks the committed `Release:` value. If it uses
the `baserelease` macro, rendering copies the committed `%global baserelease`
or `%define baserelease` line over the upstream definition without changing
the upstream `Release:` value. Missing or ambiguous definitions fail rendering.
Otherwise, it copies the committed `Release:` value. No temporary release-bump
probe is performed. An initial `%autorelease` render calculates its base release
on the host. Initial and legacy autorelease handling do not depend on version changes.
For existing output
marked by a line starting with `## RPMAUTOSPEC:` and containing `autorelease`,
an `%autorelease` upstream spec inherits the leading integer of the committed,
macro-expanded Release as `-b`. Expansion uses the committed files on the host.
Without that legacy marker, an existing `%autorelease` spec carries its
base only when the version is unchanged. For unchanged versions, it carries the
committed `-b` value into the working macro; an omitted `-b` stays omitted,
removing any working base option rather than adding `-b 1`.
An invalid numeric base fails release carryover, which requires no host release query.
When the version changes, rendering calculates the upstream numeric base and sets `-b`,
as for initial output. The committed changelog is still carried over, not regenerated.

A calculated base of `1` omits `-b`, removing any working base option while preserving
other macro options. This includes legacy expansion. Unchanged-version modern output
still carries an explicitly committed `-b 1` as-is.

The final host
`rpmdev-bumpspec -c` uses the exact `--changelog-comment` value, defaulting to
`update <component-name>`. Upstream changes are not appended to this comment.
All prepared dist-git files except `.git` are retained,
without file filtering or `spectool` queries. Synthetic history and its
legacy release adjustment are skipped during lockless rendering.
Lockless rendering never uses mock or `rpmautospec process-distgit`; the output
retains `%autorelease`, `%autochangelog`, and other RPM macros unexpanded, and
preserves the `changelog` sidecar.

When the last render's configuration has no `spec.upstream-commit`, its
committed lock file can supply the old pin if that commit's top-level
`lockless-components.txt` is absent or does not list the component. The current
render still requires a configured pin.

Rendering then replaces `{{ .RenderedSpecsDir }}/<first-char>/<name>/` and
automatically commits that directory. The first commit also includes all staged
changes, unstaged tracked changes, and non-ignored untracked files outside the
rendered output directory. Other rendered paths are excluded even if staged;
later commits include only their component's dist-git changes.
The message is the supplied changelog comment or, for non-autorelease specs,
the entry collected through `$EDITOR` or a local editor. There is no default.
Autorelease skips `rpmdev-bumpspec`; without a supplied comment, Git prompts
in its own editor for a message. The body/template contains upstream changes.
Provide `--changelog-comment` for unattended rendering.
`--no-git-commit` skips automatic staging and committing, but still renders and
bumps non-autorelease specs.
It never creates empty commits. Files outside rendered output may remain staged
if committing fails; component recovery does not reset them. Configure
Git author identity and install host `rpmdevtools`, `rpm-build`, and `rpmautospec`
as needed. `--check-only` never replaces output, creates rebuild timestamps, or commits.

For source-resolution changes, run `refresh-upstream-commit` first and review the
generated pin, then follow the commit-before-render sequence above. Do not amend
the input commit to absorb the automatically generated render commit. Local
components retain their previous rendering and manual output-commit workflow.

